# gh-checkout.dl — ghcacher's OTHER half, as a datalog program.
#
# ghcacher (~/projects/ghcacher) does two things. gh-cache.dl is the first:
# poll the GitHub API into SQLite. This is the second: keep local git CHECKOUTS
# current on disk (ghcacher's checkout.rs). For every configured repo it clones
# what is missing, fetches origin, and fast-forwards the default branch to
# origin/<branch>. A `dl --lsp` daemon on an interval IS the watch loop.
#
# `checkout(repo, branch, pr_heads)` is the built-in demand sink that runs the
# sweep. Per row:
#   * repo     = a config slug (or "." for self / a path). A config repo whose
#                root is absent but has a `url` is cloned first.
#   * branch   = the branch to keep current. Empty = discover origin/HEAD.
#   * pr_heads = "1"/"true" also mirrors +refs/pull/*/head into refs/remotes/pr/*
#                (the ghcacher fetch_pr_branches option); "0" skips it.
# On the branch it fast-forwards, dirty work is stashed and it hard-resets to
# origin/<branch>. On any OTHER branch (or detached) it moves the ref with
# `git branch -f` and leaves the working tree alone. Repos sweep in parallel;
# a failure skips loudly. `DL_NO_FETCH=1` skips the network (offline).
#
# Run (daemon):  dl --lsp examples/gh-checkout.dl --root <repo>
# One-shot:      dl examples/gh-checkout.dl --root <repo>

# --- read our own config ----------------------------------------------------
# The repo set is the built-in `repo` relation, populated from the `[[repos]]`
# config (the same config gh-cache.dl and every multi-repo program reads). Keep
# every configured repo (one with a clone `url`) current on its default branch:
# empty `branch` lets the sink follow each repo's origin/HEAD, so a repo tracking
# `master` and one tracking `main` both work from one rule.
checkout(slug, "", "0") <- repo(slug, root, url), url != "".

# Read it back: the rows that drove the sweep (one per repo kept current).
? checkout(slug, branch, pr_heads).
